## 7. Deployment

### 7.1 API Worker
1. Run `npm run api:test`.
2. Run `npm run api:deploy`, which applies remote D1 migrations and then runs `wrangler deploy`.
3. Note the URL `https://drop-ui-api.<subdomain>.workers.dev` and put it into `src/environments/environment.ts`.

**Optional:** a custom domain such as `api.<your-domain>`. Some corporate networks block `*.workers.dev`, and employers may open the demo from one.

### 7.2 Frontend on Cloudflare Pages
- Project name: `drop-ui-angular`. Production URL `https://drop-ui-angular.pages.dev`, previews at `https://<hash|branch>.drop-ui-angular.pages.dev`. The CORS pattern covers both.
- Build settings:

  | Setting | Value |
  |---|---|
  | Framework preset | None |
  | Build command | `npm run build` |
  | Output directory | `dist/drop-ui/browser` |
  | Root directory | `/` |
  | Env var | `NODE_VERSION=24` |
- Build watch paths: exclude `backend/**` so API-only commits don't trigger a frontend rebuild.
- SPA fallback is automatic: with no `404.html` in the output, Pages serves `index.html` for unknown paths.
- **Alternative**: Workers static assets. Cloudflare is steering new projects toward it. You would add a second `wrangler.jsonc` with `"assets": { "directory": "dist/drop-ui/browser", "not_found_handling": "single-page-application" }`. If you pick this, adjust the CORS preview pattern to the Workers preview URL format.
- **Same-origin option (rejected)**: Pages Functions, or a route `/api/*` on the frontend's domain, would remove CORS. But it ties the API to one frontend deployment. A separate Worker + CORS lets every framework port share one API.

### 7.3 Optional GitHub Actions
- `.github/workflows/api.yml`: on push to `main` with paths `backend/**`, run `npm ci && npm test` in `backend/`. Then `cloudflare/wrangler-action@v3` runs `d1 migrations apply drop-ui --remote` and `deploy` (`workingDirectory: backend`, secrets `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` / `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID`).
- `.github/workflows/frontend.yml`: run the three frontend suites on PRs (`npx playwright install --with-deps chromium`). Deployment itself is handled by Pages' Git integration. Alternatively, run `wrangler pages deploy dist/drop-ui/browser --project-name drop-ui-angular` from the workflow and disable the automatic builds.

### 7.4 Free-tier fit (verify all numbers on Cloudflare's pricing/limits pages before relying on them)
| Service | Free limit (approx.) | DropUI usage |
|---|---|---|
| Workers | 100,000 requests/day, 10 ms CPU per request | One `GET` per visit + one `PUT` per debounced edit. HMAC + JSON validation stays far below 10 ms |
| D1 | 5 GB total storage, ~5 M rows read/day, ~100 k rows written/day | One row read per load, one row written per save (`WITHOUT ROWID`). Max 64 KB per row |
| KV (not used) | ~1,000 writes/day, eventually consistent | Too few writes for autosave, which is why D1 was chosen |
| Pages | Unlimited static requests, ~500 builds/month | Fine |

- Nothing pauses or sleeps on inactivity, unlike Supabase free projects, which pause after about 1 week idle.
- Exceeding a daily limit returns errors until 00:00 UTC. The free plan never bills.

